        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. SNI: Many Sites, One IP"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Chicken-and-egg: cert must be sent before the Host header",
            "SNI puts the hostname in a ClientHello extension",
            "Makes name-based virtual hosting over HTTPS possible",
            "Pre-SNI, HTTPS needed one IP per certificate"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "SNI is cleartext in every version, TLS 1.3 included",
            "A passive observer sees exactly which site you visit",
            "So ISP filters, corporate firewalls and censors block on it",
            "SNI is TLS-layer cleartext; Host is HTTP-layer encrypted",
            "A mismatch is a routing bug — and old domain-fronting"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "ECH: Where The Halves Meet",
          "text": "ECH (successor to ESNI) encrypts the inner ClientHello with a key published in an HTTPS/SVCB DNS record — TLS privacy now depends on a DNS lookup that must itself be encrypted."
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Wrong or missing SNI: server serves its default cert",
            "Symptom is hostname mismatch though the cert is installed",
            "Android 2.x, IE on XP, Python < 2.7.9 send no SNI"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "openssl s_client -connect 93.184.216.34:443 \\",
            "  -servername example.com   # right cert",
            "# drop -servername: default cert served",
            "openssl x509 -in cert.pem -text -noout"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
