        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. What A Handshake Actually Costs"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Cost is round trips plus asymmetric operations",
            "RSA-2048 signing is far heavier server-side than ECDSA-P256",
            "Resumption skips certificate and signature work",
            "1.2 used session IDs (server state) or tickets (client state)",
            "1.3 uses PSK tickets from NewSessionTicket"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Rotate Ticket Keys",
          "text": "A static session-ticket key destroys forward secrecy fleet-wide: one stolen key decrypts every resumed session."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Where To Put The Crypto"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Model",
            "Gain",
            "Cost"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "terminate at LB/CDN",
              "offload, L7 routing",
              "plaintext inner hop"
            ],
            [
              "re-encrypt",
              "both",
              "double CPU"
            ],
            [
              "passthrough",
              "true end-to-end",
              "steer on SNI only"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "ALPN negotiates h2 / http1.1 / h3 during the handshake",
            "Saves an extra protocol upgrade round trip",
            "QUIC/HTTP-3 folds TLS 1.3 into the transport over UDP",
            "Connection reuse beats every handshake trick",
            "Keep-alive pays the handshake per connection, not per request"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Every extra intermediate is more bytes in the first flight",
            "Long chains can overflow the initial congestion window",
            "ChaCha20-Poly1305 beats AES without AES-NI hardware",
            "Which is why mobile clients often prefer it"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "cont": true
